-- title:
--   Complex roots of unity lie in the image of σ
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field that is algebraically closed and of characteristic zero, and let $\sigma\colon F \to \mathbb{C}$ be a ring homomorphism. Let $\zeta \in \mathbb{C}$ and let $n$ be a natural number with $0 < n$, and suppose $\zeta^{n} = 1$. The conclusion is that $\zeta$ lies in the range of $\sigma$, that is, $\zeta = \sigma(\xi)$ for some $\xi \in F$. Thus every root of unity of $\mathbb{C}$ — of any positive order — belongs to the image of an arbitrary ring homomorphism from an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero into $\mathbb{C}$; no surjectivity, continuity or algebraicity hypothesis on $\sigma$ is needed, and the order $n$ is not assumed to be the exact order of $\zeta$, only an exponent killing it.
--
--   This is the standard fact that the group of $n$-th roots of unity in $\mathbb{C}$ is generated by the image of a primitive $n$-th root of unity of $F$, used with $F$ an algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ equipped with a complex embedding, so that vectors with cyclotomic coordinates may be pulled back to $F$. It is invoked in the estimates [`ModularCurve.JZero.prox_sum_le_of_forall_log_secVal_le`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JZero.prox_sum_le_of_forall_log_secVal_le) and [`Pencil.exists_covector_sum_log_ge_of_ringHom`](thm.html#Pencil.exists_covector_sum_log_ge_of_ringHom).
